@@ -0,0 +1,111 @@
+# Building OrcaCubic on FreeBSD
+
+This is a native system-library build for FreeBSD 13.5 or later. It has been
+verified with Clang 19, CMake 3.31, Ninja 1.13, wxWidgets 3.2/GTK3, Boost 1.89,
+OpenCASCADE 7.9, and Python 3.11.
+
+## Dependencies
+
+Install the development packages through FreeBSD packages or ports. The tested
+package set includes:
+
+```sh
+pkg install cmake ninja llvm19 boost-libs python311 wx32-gtk3 openvdb \
+    cgal eigen libnoise hidapi libsecret webkit2-gtk_41 glfw glew assimp \
+    nlopt onetbb gmp mpfr curl
+```
+
+Additional GUI/media dependencies are pulled in by wxWidgets and WebKitGTK on
+the tested package set. `wxInspector` is not packaged on FreeBSD: the build
+downloads OrcaCubic's pinned v1.0.0 source archive and applies the tracked
+wxWidgets 3.2 compatibility patch. Nothing is installed outside the build
+directory.
+
+FreeBSD uses the selected system Python development runtime rather than the
+Windows/macOS bundled Python distribution. No `pip` packages are required for
+the bundled Kobra X LAN bridge. The plugin loader recognizes FreeBSD native
+wheel platform tags for the selected Python ABI; third-party plugin
+compatibility still depends on the plugin and its available wheels.
+Installing a plugin's extra Python packages requires `uv`; this tested host
+does not have it. That optional feature can be enabled by configuring with
+`-DORCA_BUNDLED_UV_EXECUTABLE=/path/to/uv` after obtaining a FreeBSD-compatible
+`uv` executable. The Kobra X bridge and native slicer build do not need `uv`.
+
+The bridge's token-file handling, local HTTP authentication, signed HTTP/AES
+key exchange, MQTT credential/setup and connection status, and HTTP
+upload/MQTT start-message formatting have loopback-only or mocked regression
+tests that do not contact a printer.
+The mock upload rejection test also verifies that no start message is
+published after a rejected upload:
+
+```sh
+env PYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE=1 python3 -m unittest \
+    resources/scripts/tests/test_anycubic_bridge.py -v
+```
+
+The bridge can be smoke-tested without a printer by running it with a
+loopback-only address and a disposable token, then requesting
+`http://127.0.0.1:18988/health` from another terminal. An unauthenticated
+request to `/status` should return HTTP 403. Stop the bridge after the test;
+this checks Python startup and local authentication, not a Kobra X connection.
+The GUI launcher instead creates an owner-only token file in its data directory
+and passes its path to the bridge, so the live token does not appear in the
+bridge command line. Keep that data directory private.
+
+```sh
+env PYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE=1 ORCACUBIC_BRIDGE_TOKEN=local-smoke-only \
+    python3 resources/scripts/anycubic_lan_daemon.py 127.0.0.1
+# In another terminal while the bridge is running:
+curl http://127.0.0.1:18988/health
+```
+
+## Configure and build
+
+Use an out-of-source build:
+
+```sh
+cmake -S . -B build-freebsd -G Ninja \
+    -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release \
+    -DSLIC3R_STATIC=OFF \
+    -DSLIC3R_GUI=ON \
+    -DSLIC3R_GTK=3 \
+    -DOPENVDB_FIND_MODULE_PATH=/usr/local/lib/cmake/OpenVDB \
+    -DBUILD_TESTS=OFF -Wno-dev
+cmake --build build-freebsd --target OrcaSlicer --parallel 4
+```
+
+The resulting executable is `build-freebsd/src/orca-slicer`. It dynamically
+links against system libraries, including the FreeBSD Python runtime. Before
+using existing profiles, first start it in a graphical session with a separate
+data directory:
+
+```sh
+build-freebsd/src/orca-slicer --datadir /path/to/empty-orcacubic-data
+```
+
+With the default install prefix, `cmake --install build-freebsd` stages the
+application and resources privately under `build-freebsd/OrcaSlicer/`; it does
+not install a FreeBSD package or replace system libraries. The staged GUI can
+be started as `build-freebsd/OrcaSlicer/bin/orca-slicer --datadir
+/path/to/empty-orcacubic-data`. This staged layout was tested on FreeBSD 13.5.
+
+Do not point this initial run at an existing OrcaSlicer or Cura configuration.
+Once the GUI starts, use its import workflow to copy profiles rather than
+moving or overwriting source profiles. OrcaSlicer user preset `.json` and
+`.info` pairs can also be copied into the new data directory's
+`user/default/<preset-type>/` folder after the corresponding system printer
+has been enabled. Keep Cura configurations separate: Cura's `.cfg` format is
+not directly importable as an OrcaCubic preset.
+
+## Current validation boundary
+
+The native binary builds and launches its GUI on FreeBSD 13.5/GTK3. A copied
+OrcaSlicer filament preset and locally converted Cura printer/process/filament
+presets were loaded and selected with an isolated `--datadir`; a small model
+also sliced successfully through the command-line path. With a loopback-only
+Kobra X test profile, the GUI launched and stopped the bundled Python bridge
+correctly. These tests do not establish physical print safety. A full GUI
+slice, Kobra X LAN handshake, OctoPrint connection, and printer-side
+validation remain pending. In particular, do not send command-line test
+G-code to a printer unless the full machine profile and its inherited
+settings were loaded and reviewed.
